{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 8 &middot; a data-fed site</p>
    <h1>Where to buy Aster &amp; Oak</h1>
    <p>
      Every shop below is one JSON file in <code>models/stockists</code>, exported from the
      trade CRM each morning. The site is a fan-out over that folder: one page per record,
      one view, one controller that checks the record before it is allowed to become a page.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="grid">
    {{#each model.stocked}}
    <article class="card">
      <h3><a href="stockists/{{slug}}.html">{{name}}</a></h3>
      <p class="meta">{{town}} &middot; stocking us since {{since}}</p>
      <p>{{note}}</p>
      <p>{{#each sells}}<span class="tag">{{this}}</span> {{/each}}</p>
    </article>
    {{/each}}
  </div>

  {{#if model.incomplete}}
  <div class="callout">
    <h2>One record in this feed is broken on purpose</h2>
    <p class="tone-warn">
      {{#each model.incomplete}}
      <strong>{{name}}</strong> (feed key <code>{{key}}</code>) has no <code>{{missing}}</code>.
      {{/each}}
    </p>
    <p>
      It is the shape of breakage a real feed produces: the row was created the day the pitch
      was agreed and a required field was never filled in. The controller refuses to build a
      stockist page with no address, so that one page fails, the other five build, and the
      command exits 1 naming the record:
    </p>
    <pre><code>1 page(s) failed to build: stockists/stockist.hbs [item 3: harbour-market-stall]
  stockists/stockist.hbs [item 3: harbour-market-stall]: Incomplete stockist record — missing address.</code></pre>
    <p>
      The listing you are reading knows the same thing for the same reason — it reports against
      the validator the controller enforces, so it names the gap rather than silently dropping a shop.
    </p>
  </div>
  {{/if}}

  <div class="card">
    {{> "newsletter"}}
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    <strong>The fan-out.</strong> <code>.pages()</code> is <code>.page()</code> with an array.
    Point <code>model</code> at the <code>models/stockists</code> folder and kiss loads every
    JSON file in it, then builds one page per record from <code>stockists/stockist.hbs</code>.
    <code>path: 'stockists'</code> puts them in a folder; the controller's <code>slug</code>
    decides the filename.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The validation.</strong> <code>controllers/stockist.js</code> is the only place the
    feed's contract is written down. It throws for a record that cannot make a correct page, and
    exports the same check so the index can report on it. A throw fails one item, not the
    fan-out: every other record is still registered and built, and each bad one is reported
    separately as <code>&lt;view&gt; [item N: &lt;slug&gt;]</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The exit code.</strong> The script <code>await</code>s <code>complete()</code> and
    catches its rejection. Without that a broken feed exits 0 and a deploy ships a site with a
    hole in it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The build.</strong> Run it with <code>--atomic</code> and the same failure publishes
    nothing at all: <code>cleanBuild: 'atomic'</code> stages the whole site and swaps it in only
    when <code>complete()</code> resolves, so a bad feed can never half-replace a live site.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
